invariant: INV-26
file: ghash_hw.c
catches: ghash_equiv_test
reason: the AES=hw GHASH pass multiplies its later blocks by the powers of
  H in the wrong order, the second block by H^2 where a pass of count
  blocks needs H^(count - 1), so every pass of two blocks or more hashes
  to the wrong value. It is the mistake an index that counts the other way
  invites. CBMC cannot read the instruction, so bin/ghash_equiv_test is
  what holds it: it runs every data length through three passes of eight
  blocks against gcm.c's portable loop, one block at a time. The edit is
  shared C after either instruction, so it lands on an ARMv8 runner and an
  x86-64 one alike.

--- old
        multiply_accumulate(&s->sums, block_vector(&data[j * AES_BLOCK]), s->powers[count - 1 - j]);
--- new
        multiply_accumulate(&s->sums, block_vector(&data[j * AES_BLOCK]), s->powers[j]);
